// Logos standard library — logos.lang.stream
//
// The batch-stream vocabulary of ADR 0025 (`docs/adr/0025-deem-batch-cursor-plane.md`):
// one logical data representation for query execution — a stream of bounded
// batches of typed rows — plus the orthogonal capability traits that record
// what a stream can do.
//
// WHY IT LIVES AT THE LANGUAGE TIER (ADR 0025 §11 [REC]): these are
// general-language notions, the batched sibling of `Iterator`
// (`logos.lang.iter`) the way `ExactSizeIterator` is a sibling in Rust core.
// Rooting them in one supplier (`logos.mem.bt.map`, the query plane, a single
// container family) is the recorded "vocabulary rooted in one supplier"
// defect — every new source would re-declare its own spelling and every
// consumer would branch on which one it holds. The query plane CONSUMES this
// module; it does not own it.
//
// The lang tier is no-alloc/no-OS (`stdlib/lang/logos.module`, tier `lang`),
// so only the protocol, the capabilities and the BORROWED batch view live
// here. `Buffer<R>` — the degenerate one-packet stream over a `Vec` (ADR §4)
// — needs the heap and therefore sits one tier up in `logos.mem.stream`
// (`stdlib/mem/stream/buffer.logos`).
//
// Not in the prelude on purpose: `BatchStream::next` and `Iterator::next` have
// the same name and receiver shape, so putting this module in the implicit
// prelude would make `it.next()` ambiguous for every iterator in the tree.
// Consumers write `use logos.lang.stream;`.

package logos.lang.stream;

use logos.lang.option;

// ── The pull protocol (ADR 0025 §1) ─────────────────────────────────────────
//
// The pull unit is a BATCH, not a row. Row-at-a-time is a CONSUMER pattern
// (the inner loop), not the protocol:
//
//     while let Option::Some(b) = s.next() {
//         let n: i64 = b.len() as i64;
//         let mut i: i64 = 0 as i64;
//         while i < n { /* clauses over b @ i */ i = i + (1 as i64); }
//     }
//
// A heap slice is a stream of EXACTLY ONE batch — the whole `&[T]` — so the
// old indexed loop is literally the inner loop of the degenerate case.

/// A stream of bounded batches of typed rows: the one pull protocol of ADR 0025.
///
/// `next` returns:
/// * `Option::None` — the stream is EXHAUSTED. Terminal: no further call is defined
///   unless the stream is also `Rewind` and has been rewound.
/// * `Option::Some(b)` with `b.len() == 0` — a LEGAL tick, not an end: an empty leaf,
///   an empty epoch (ADR §10), a no-progress/keepalive tick of a budgeted producer.
///   A consumer must treat it as "no rows this time, ask again", never as termination.
///
/// The batch `B` is a typed VIEW over storage (`RowsBatch<R>` row-major, `ColsBatch`
/// columnar), never a copy, and never `dyn` (§7b): the inner loop over a batch is
/// monomorphic and inlined, so no dispatch sits at per-row frequency.
pub trait BatchStream<B> {
    /// Pulls the next batch; `None` = exhausted, `Some(empty)` = a legal no-rows tick.
    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<B>;
}

// ── Capabilities (ADR 0025 §3) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Capabilities are ORTHOGONAL TRAITS on the stream type, not shapes. Existence
// of a capability = trait membership, asked by the planner via metaprog trait
// queries: a declaration can lie about code, a type cannot (the S4c lesson).
// Cost, exactness and the operation set stay DECLARATIONS (`op … exact`,
// `size <rel> = <fn>`, ADR 0024 S6) because cost is data-dependent and
// inexpressible as a type.

/// The stream can be re-landed and replayed from the start: multi-pass.
///
/// Consuming a non-`Rewind` stream twice is a plan-level error whose repair is an
/// inserted `Drain` with its ground (§4). S2j put the DRAIN in place — the planner
/// inserts the node where its single-pass proof used to be recorded
/// (`wql::plan_walker::plan_insert_drains`) — so what this trait still owes is the
/// REFUSAL: today a second read is caught by a planner walking the query, and it
/// should be caught by the type of the thing being read twice.
pub trait Rewind {
    /// Re-lands the stream at the beginning; the next `next()` yields the first batch again.
    fn rewind(&mut self);
}

/// The stream knows its row count for free — without draining it.
///
/// Free means O(1)-ish and side-effect-free: a declared container `size`, a `Vec`
/// length. A source that would have to walk to answer must NOT implement this.
pub trait SizedStream {
    /// Total number of rows the stream will yield, without consuming it.
    fn size(&self) -> u64;
}

/// Marker: batches arrive in `K` order, and each batch is sorted by `K` internally.
///
/// The order fact that today's ten representations record nowhere (family walks and
/// `BTreeMapIter` have it physically; `HashMapIter` does not). Immediate harvest:
/// an `order by` over a column whose source already declares this order is a NO-OP.
/// Carries no method: it is a promise about the `next()` sequence, checked by the
/// producer's own tests, not by a signature.
pub trait OrderedBy<K> {}

/// Ordered positioning: the stream can jump to key `K` (the landing constructors' shape).
///
/// Only meaningful together with `OrderedBy<K>` — seeking a stream with no `K` order
/// is a request nothing can answer. `seek_key` past the last key leaves the stream
/// exhausted (the next `next()` is `None`), never a panic.
///
/// NAMED `seek_key`, NOT `seek` (S1, 2026-08-13). The generated container beneath a
/// family walk already spells the two positionings `seek(n: u64)` = BY ORDINAL and
/// `seek_key(k)` = BY KEY, and a family walk implements `Landed<K>` and
/// `RandomAccess<B>` SIMULTANEOUSLY — so a `Landed::seek(k)` would have put the
/// container's two conventions on one type INVERTED (`s.seek(k)` by key beside
/// `s.seek_nth(n)` by ordinal, over a `c.seek(n)` by ordinal one layer down). The
/// rename was the cheaper of the two repairs the hazard admits — the other being a
/// doc-comment at both sites — and it was cheap precisely because `Landed` had ONE
/// impl (the emitted `{N}LeafWalk`) and one caller when S1 opened. It stops being
/// cheap once S3 consumes the trait.
pub trait Landed<K> {
    /// Positions the stream at the first row with key `>= k`. By KEY — the ordinal
    /// twin is `RandomAccess::seek_nth`.
    fn seek_key(&mut self, k: K);
}

// ── The TRAVERSAL axis (ADR 0025 §3, Victor 2026-08-11) ─────────────────────
//
// A THIRD orthogonal capability axis beside order-as-fact (`OrderedBy<K>`) and
// key-positioning (`Landed<K>`):
//
//     forward-only ⊂ bidirectional ⊂ random-access (by ordinal)
//
// and the ⊂ is the SUPERTRAIT EDGE below, so the lattice lives in the type
// system and cannot drift from the table that describes it. Concretely:
//
//   * forward-only  = a plain `BatchStream` with NEITHER trait. No third name is
//     minted for the bottom of the chain — that is the recorded "`can_seek` as
//     one bool is WRONG" lesson applied in the other direction: the absence of a
//     capability is not a capability. `HashMapIter` sits here (and is not on this
//     plane at all today — it implements `Iterator`, not `BatchStream`).
//   * bidirectional = `Bidirectional<B>`. A btree walk.
//   * random-access = `RandomAccess<B>`, which IMPLIES `Bidirectional<B>`. Family
//     walks, heap slices and `Buffer`.
//
// The S3 harvest this vocabulary is FOR: `offset`/`limit` push down as one
// `seek_nth` instead of a skip loop; `order by … desc` over a bidirectional
// source emits no `Sort`; ordinal sampling needs no drain.
//
// ⚠ WHAT LANDED, AND THE ONE THAT COULD NOT — measured, not planned around:
//
//   * `order by … desc` emits no `Sort`: LANDED (S3-desc). The emitted backward
//     scan opens with `land_end()`, which delegates to `RandomAccess::seek_nth`
//     — so `seek_nth` HAS a wql consumer, reached through the DIRECTION axis.
//     An earlier reading of this section recorded that it had none and could
//     not acquire one; that is superseded, and by a different route than the
//     one predicted.
//   * `limit` as a bounded walk: LANDED, with no new mechanism — the streamed
//     arm's existing break, now also over the reversed walk, which is `top-n`.
//   * `offset` as one `seek_nth` instead of a skip loop: NO SURFACE, RECORDED
//     RATHER THAN INVENTED. There is no `offset` keyword in `wql.peg`, no field
//     in `plan.logos`, no node in `lower.logos` and no fixture — `grep -i
//     offset` over `stdlib/mem/wql/` yields only `byte_offset`, the column
//     window `rel_coff` and srcloc. So THERE IS NO SKIP LOOP TO REPLACE,
//     because there is no way to ask for one. The ordinal landing this trait
//     exists for is real and measured (the leaf-descent gate pins the landing
//     at ZERO descents); what is missing is a query surface that asks for it by
//     ordinal. `where <key> >= k … limit n` already lands by KEY through the
//     ops plane in one descent, which is the same harvest for the cases the
//     language can currently spell.

/// Bidirectional traversal: batches can be pulled BACKWARD as well as forward.
///
/// `prev` returns:
/// * `Option::None` — the stream is AT THE START. The exact mirror of `next`'s
///   `None` = exhausted: a terminal answer for this direction, not an error.
/// * `Option::Some(b)` — the batch immediately BEFORE the current position, and the
///   position moves to that batch's first row. An EMPTY batch stays legal in this
///   direction too (§1), for the same reasons.
///
/// Alternating `next`/`prev` therefore hands out the same batch twice, which is the
/// point: `prev` un-consumes, it does not skip.
pub trait Bidirectional<B> {
    /// Pulls the batch before the current position; `None` = already at the start.
    fn prev(&mut self) -> Option<B>;
}

/// Random access BY ENTRY ORDINAL (ADR 0025 §3).
///
/// Every Memoria container is an ARRAY of its entries, so positioning by ordinal is
/// ONE DESCENT — which is exactly what keeps §7b's per-LEAF frequency invariant: no
/// dyn call and no seek is paid per ROW. That is why this is a capability of the
/// stream rather than an adapter that skips.
///
/// POSITIONING IS EXACT AND 0-BASED OVER THE STREAM'S OWN LANDING, not over the
/// container: a narrowed landing's ordinal 0 is ITS first row, not the container's.
/// `seek_nth(n)` with `n` past the landing's last row leaves the stream exhausted
/// (the next `next()` is `None`), never a panic — the same contract `Landed::seek_key`
/// states for a key past the end.
///
/// `size()` (`SizedStream`) is the LANDING's total and is NOT changed by `seek_nth`:
/// seeking moves a POSITION inside a landing, it does not re-land. Re-landing is
/// `Landed::seek_key` (by key) or `Rewind::rewind` (to the start).
pub trait RandomAccess<B>: Bidirectional<B> {
    /// Positions the stream so the next `next()` yields the batch containing row `n`,
    /// counted from the stream's own landing. One descent, never a skip loop.
    fn seek_nth(&mut self, n: u64);
}

// ── The row-major batch (ADR 0025 §2) ───────────────────────────────────────
//
// THE BARE SLICE IS THE BATCH. §2 spells the row-major batch `RowsBatch<R>` =
// `&[R]`, and in Logos that equality can be literal rather than a wrapper:
// `&[R]` already carries `len()` and `[i]`, which is the whole batch surface
// the emitted scan loop uses. A struct wrapper would add a field to unwrap at
// every access, a lifetime knot to re-tie, and a second spelling of `len` —
// paying for nothing. So `RowsBatch<R>` is an ALIAS, and
// `impl BatchStream<&[R]> for …` / `impl BatchStream<RowsBatch<R>> for …` are
// the same impl (both spellings measured compiling, 2026-08-11).
//
// The alias still earns its place: it is the name the emitter and the ADR use,
// and it is where a future selection-vector or bounds-carrying row batch would
// land WITHOUT touching the impls that spell it by name (selection vectors are
// deliberately out of v1 — recorded as an axis).

/// The row-major batch of ADR 0025 §2: a borrowed window of `R` rows.
///
/// Literally `&[R]` — the slice IS the batch (`len()` + `[i]` is the whole surface
/// the scan loop needs), so heap collections, fixpoint outputs and deltas hand out
/// their storage directly and the batch costs nothing to build. The columnar
/// counterpart (`ColsBatch`/`PdtCol` over a Memoria leaf's PdtBuf slots) is a
/// separate type, selected by the source's declaration, not by a branch.
pub type RowsBatch<R> = &[R];

/// Marker: this stream's batches are ROW-MAJOR (`RowsBatch<R>` = `&[R]`), not columnar.
///
/// ADR 0025 §2 says the layout is "declaration-selected", and S6 is where the second
/// layout acquired a consumer, so this is where the selector had to become real. It is
/// a MARKER TRAIT ON THE STREAM TYPE for the same reason `OrderedBy`/`Bidirectional`
/// are: the planner asks the producer's RETURN TYPE, and a type cannot lie about what
/// it implements, while a `layout = rows` declaration beside the producer could.
///
/// WHY IT IS A SEPARATE TRAIT AND NOT READ OFF `BatchStream<B>`'s ARGUMENT — which is
/// where the answer physically is (`BatchStream<RowsBatch<R>>` vs
/// `BatchStream<ColsBatch>`): `sema_has_impl_recursive` answers MEMBERSHIP with a bool
/// and hands back no trait type arguments. That is the same recorded residual the
/// `order <rel> = <col>` declaration works around (`sema_expr.cpp`, the `o` letter),
/// and closing it would let this marker be deleted in favour of the argument — noted
/// here so the marker retires with the residual rather than outliving it.
///
/// Absence means COLUMNAR: the family leaf walk (`ColsBatch` over a Memoria leaf's
/// PdtBuf slots) is the layout the emitter spelled first and still spells by default,
/// and every producer that predates this marker is one.
pub trait RowMajor {}

// ── SliceStream — THE BARE `&[R]`, READ AS §1's ONE SHAPE (ADR 0025 R-A) ─────
//
// The type that makes "a heap slice is a stream of EXACTLY ONE batch" (§1's own
// sentence, at the top of this file) a TYPE rather than a remark. Without it
// the emitter had two scan shapes and picked between them by asking whether the
// source had a rel-registry index: a `deem` SLICE PARAM (`rows: &[Row]`) has
// none — `SemaChecker::native_source_spec` returns `{}` for a param type with
// no `impl Src`, so no natspec entry is written, `register_native_rels`
// registers no rel, and `rel_find_src` answers −1 — and every such query
// therefore emitted the old indexed walk. THIS is the wrap that gives the
// paramslice a batch door WITHOUT giving it a rel identity (the "param rels"
// route, which moves the census and every `ri`-keyed author, stays owed).
//
// NO ALLOCATION, NO COPY, and that is why it lives at the LANG tier beside the
// traits rather than up in `logos.mem.stream` with `Buffer<R>`: it BORROWS
// (`#[borrow_carrying]`, `rows: &[R]`) and owns nothing, so it needs no heap.
// The refuted alternative is on the record: wrapping a param slice as
// `Buffer::from_vec` COPIES every row into a fresh `Vec` — an intermediate
// materialization the caller never asked for, which is exactly what criterion 1
// forbids, and which the S2j byte-pin measured as the losing route.
//
// ⚠ IT IS `Epoch<R>` MINUS THE WEIGHT — said here rather than re-minted in
// silence. `Epoch<R>` (below) is `SliceStream<R>` plus one `i64`: same
// `#[borrow_carrying]` one-packet shape, same `next_batch()` inherent door,
// same four impls. They are kept as SIBLINGS at R-A because that is the
// smaller step and it does not move `Epoch`'s bytes; expressing `Epoch` OVER
// `SliceStream` (`Epoch = { s: SliceStream<R>, w: i64 }`) is the step that
// stops the two from drifting, and it is OWED, not done.

/// A borrowed `&[R]` served as ONE batch: ADR 0025 §1's degenerate stream, no-alloc.
///
/// Yields the whole slice as a single `RowsBatch<R>`, then `None`. `Buffer<R>`
/// (`logos.mem.stream`) is the OWNING twin — what a `Drain`/`Sort` materialization
/// produces; `SliceStream<R>` is what rows the caller ALREADY holds become, at zero
/// cost, so a `deem` query over a slice param rides the same emitted scan as a
/// Memoria leaf walk instead of a second shape.
///
/// THE EMPTY SLICE IS A TICK: a `SliceStream` over `&[]` yields ONE `Some(empty)`
/// before `None`, the same rule `Buffer` and `Epoch` follow — "no rows" and "over"
/// are different answers (§1) and an emitted scan that folded them would run its
/// outer body a different number of times for an empty source.
///
/// Implements `RowMajor` (the batch IS `&[R]`), `Rewind` (free — the rows are the
/// caller's and were never consumed) and `SizedStream` (the length is the slice's).
/// DELIBERATELY NOT `OrderedBy<K>`: a bare slice carries no order fact, and the
/// traversal chain (`Bidirectional`/`RandomAccess`) is left off for the reason
/// `Epoch` leaves it off — nothing positions inside a one-packet param scan today,
/// and a capability with no consumer is a claim no test can bite. The random-access
/// face the sort path needs is `as_slice()`, an inherent method, not a capability.
#[borrow_carrying]
pub struct SliceStream<R> {
    /// The caller's rows, borrowed for the life of the stream.
    rows: &[R],
    /// False until the single packet has been handed out; `rewind` clears it.
    sent: bool,
}

impl<R> SliceStream<R> {
    /// Wraps a borrowed slice as a one-packet stream. Copies nothing.
    pub fn new(rows: &[R]) -> SliceStream<R> {
        return SliceStream { rows: rows, sent: false };
    }

    /// Pulls the single packet — THE INHERENT DOOR, the one a generated scan calls.
    ///
    /// `rexpr_walk::batch_scan_frag` (§1's one scan shape) spells the outer pull
    /// `next_batch()` and not `BatchStream::next`: the trait method shares its name and
    /// receiver shape with `Iterator::next`, which is why `logos.lang.stream` is out of
    /// the prelude, and a generated chunk's import list must not have to carry the
    /// trait to scan. Same reasoning as `Buffer::next_batch` and `Epoch::next_batch`.
    pub fn next_batch(&mut self) -> Option<RowsBatch<R>> {
        if self.sent { return Option::None; }
        self.sent = true;
        return Option::Some(self.rows);
    }

    /// The rows as a slice, independent of the stream position.
    ///
    /// The RANDOM-ACCESS face of the same borrow, for the sites that index rather than
    /// scan — the sort path's phase 3 projects rows in PERMUTATION order
    /// (`rows[perm[o]]`), which is not a pull at all. Inherent rather than
    /// `RandomAccess<B>`: `seek_nth` positions a stream, and phase 3 does not have a
    /// position, it has a list of ordinals.
    pub fn as_slice(&self) -> &[R] {
        return self.rows;
    }
}

// One packet, then exhaustion — including the EMPTY slice, which still ticks once.
impl<R> BatchStream<RowsBatch<R>> for SliceStream<R> {
    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<RowsBatch<R>> { return self.next_batch(); }
}

// Row-major: the batch is `&[R]`, the caller's own slice (§2). This is what makes
// `batch_row_text` spell the row as `bb[j]` instead of `bb.<col>_at(j)`.
impl<R> RowMajor for SliceStream<R> {}

// Re-presenting the same slice is free — the rows are the caller's and were never
// consumed. A slice param is therefore MULTI-PASS by type, which is the fact the
// plan's read-once proof used to have to carry for it.
impl<R> Rewind for SliceStream<R> {
    fn rewind(&mut self) {
        self.sent = false;
    }
}

// The row count is the slice length — free, and unmoved by pulling.
impl<R> SizedStream for SliceStream<R> {
    fn size(&self) -> u64 {
        return self.rows.len() as u64;
    }
}

// ── The DBSP seam (ADR 0025 §10) ────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// A delta stream is a batch stream; an EPOCH IS ONE BATCH; an empty epoch is
// `Some(empty)` — a tick, not an end (§1's rule, applied to the incremental
// tier). What §10 records `[REC]` is the constraint the two types below are
// shaped by: THE WEIGHT DOES NOT ENTER THE CORE ROW MODEL. An ordinary query
// pays nothing for the incremental tier's existence, and that is a MEASURED
// claim, not an intention — the whole-corpus artifact snapshot is byte-
// identical across the stage that added this vocabulary (168 dumps /
// 7,688,158 bytes, `diff -rq` empty, 2026-08-14).
//
// SO THE WEIGHT LIVES AT THE BOUNDARY, IN TWO SPELLINGS, AND THE SPLIT IS
// FORCED BY THE TYPES rather than chosen for symmetry:
//
//   * `Epoch<R>` — ONE weight for the whole packet, over BORROWED rows. This is
//     the incremental tier's surface as it exists today: the emitted
//     `<q>_apply(&mut h, __src: i32, __wd: i64, src: &[R])` takes a scalar
//     `__wd` and a slice, and `<q>_epoch` / `<q>_retract` are pure forwarders
//     at +1 / −1 (`wql::rexpr_walk`, "P2: THE DELTA CARRIES (SOURCE, WEIGHT,
//     ROWS)"). `Epoch` is that triple minus the tag, as a stream: it costs no
//     allocation because the rows stay where the caller had them.
//   * `Weighted<R>` + `DeltaBatch<R>` — a weight PER ROW: the strict
//     generalization, the one packet that mixes +1 and −1 rows, which the
//     scalar surface cannot express at all (today such an epoch must be split
//     into two `_apply` calls). `Buffer<Weighted<R>>` (`logos.mem.stream`) is
//     already a delta stream of this shape with no new type: that is z⁻¹ and
//     DBSP's arranged input, spelled with what S0 landed.
//
// ⚠ WHY `Epoch<R>` CANNOT SIMPLY HAND OUT `DeltaBatch<R>`, which is the reason
// BOTH exist rather than only the general one: `&[R]` and `&[Weighted<R>]` are
// different layouts, so wrapping a borrowed constant-weight delta per row is a
// MATERIALIZATION (a `Vec<Weighted<R>>` the caller never asked for) — exactly
// the intermediate materialization criterion 1 exists to forbid. The
// constant-weight packet therefore keeps its weight BESIDE the batch, and a
// producer that genuinely mixes signs hands out the per-row form it built
// anyway.
//
// ⚠ WHAT IS VOCABULARY AND WHAT IS CONSUMED — stated here because a type with
// no consumer that reads as landed is the recorded "green over a branch that
// never executed" shape. `Epoch`/`Weighted` are consumed by hand-written
// consumers (the boundary fixture `stream_weighted_epoch_seam`), NOT by the
// emitter: `<q>_apply` still takes `(__src, __wd, &[R])`. The measured reason
// is in ADR §10 — the delta parameter list is the QUERY's parameter list
// verbatim, shared with the batch form `<q>()`, so re-spelling the delta as a
// stream re-spells the ordinary query's signature too; and DRed's four phases
// (`_od`/`_odp`/`_cpt`/`_i`) take `&[RowTy]` of DERIVED facts whose sign is
// fixed by PHASE, not by a row. The consumption seam is the plane's declared
// next step, named at `<q>_apply`'s parameter list.

/// A row carrying its Z-set multiplicity: the per-row half of the DBSP seam (§10).
///
/// `w > 0` = insertions (a multiset row of multiplicity `w`), `w < 0` = retractions,
/// `w == 0` = a row that cancelled and which a normalized delta does not carry at all.
///
/// A WRAPPER, never a field on `R`: the row type stored by the incremental tier
/// (`__s<k>: Vec<R>`, `__edb: Vec<R>`) and the row type an ordinary query scans are the
/// SAME type, and the weight rides beside it — in a sidecar vector in stored state, in
/// this wrapper at the boundary. That is §10's `[REC]` made structural.
pub struct Weighted<R> {
    /// The row itself, unweighted and unchanged — the core row model.
    pub row: R,
    /// The Z-set multiplicity: +1 insert, −1 retract, `n` for `n` copies.
    pub w: i64,
}

impl<R> Weighted<R> {
    /// Pairs a row with an explicit multiplicity.
    pub fn at(row: R, w: i64) -> Weighted<R> {
        return Weighted { row: row, w: w };
    }

    /// One insertion: weight +1 — what `<q>_epoch` forwards at.
    pub fn insert(row: R) -> Weighted<R> {
        return Weighted { row: row, w: 1i64 };
    }

    /// One retraction: weight −1 — what `<q>_retract` forwards at.
    pub fn retract(row: R) -> Weighted<R> {
        return Weighted { row: row, w: -1i64 };
    }
}

/// The per-row-weight delta batch: `&[Weighted<R>]` (§2's row-major batch over §10's row).
///
/// The general form a delta stream hands out — `BatchStream<DeltaBatch<R>>` — and the only
/// spelling in which ONE packet can carry both signs.
pub type DeltaBatch<R> = RowsBatch<Weighted<R>>;

/// ONE EPOCH AS ONE BATCH, at ONE weight, over rows the caller still owns (§10).
///
/// The stream form of the incremental tier's existing door: `<q>_epoch(h, src)` is
/// `Epoch::insert(src)`, `<q>_retract(h, src)` is `Epoch::retract(src)`, and the
/// general `<q>_apply(h, tag, w, src)` is `Epoch::at(src, w)` plus the tag. Yields the
/// whole delta as one `RowsBatch<R>`, then `None`.
///
/// THE EMPTY EPOCH IS A TICK: an `Epoch` over an empty slice yields ONE `Some(empty)`
/// before `None`, exactly as `Buffer` does, because "this epoch changed nothing" and
/// "this stream is over" are different answers and a consumer that folds them loses the
/// clock. Pinned by `stream_weighted_epoch_seam`.
///
/// BORROWS ITS ROWS (`#[borrow_carrying]`), unlike `Buffer<R>` which owns them: a delta
/// is presented by the caller for the duration of one apply and is not state the tier
/// keeps — the state it keeps is the arrangement (`Buffer`, z⁻¹), which owns.
///
/// ⚠ `Epoch<R> == SliceStream<R> + w`, field for field and impl for impl. R-A minted the
/// unweighted sibling above rather than re-expressing this type over it, so this is the
/// place the equivalence is written down: the two must move together, and folding
/// `Epoch` into `{ s: SliceStream<R>, w: i64 }` is the recorded step that would make the
/// drift impossible instead of merely noticed.
#[borrow_carrying]
pub struct Epoch<R> {
    /// The delta's rows, borrowed from the caller for this epoch.
    rows: &[R],
    /// The one weight the whole packet carries: +1 insert, −1 retract, `n` copies.
    w: i64,
    /// False until the single packet has been handed out; `rewind` clears it.
    sent: bool,
}

impl<R> Epoch<R> {
    /// An epoch of `rows` at an explicit weight — the general `<q>_apply` form.
    pub fn at(rows: &[R], w: i64) -> Epoch<R> {
        return Epoch { rows: rows, w: w, sent: false };
    }

    /// An INSERT epoch: every row at +1 (`<q>_epoch`).
    pub fn insert(rows: &[R]) -> Epoch<R> {
        return Epoch { rows: rows, w: 1i64, sent: false };
    }

    /// A RETRACT epoch: every row at −1 (`<q>_retract`).
    pub fn retract(rows: &[R]) -> Epoch<R> {
        return Epoch { rows: rows, w: -1i64, sent: false };
    }

    /// The packet's weight — the scalar `__wd` the emitted fold multiplies into.
    pub fn weight(&self) -> i64 {
        return self.w;
    }

    /// Pulls the single packet — THE INHERENT DOOR, the one a generated scan calls
    /// (`batch_scan_frag` spells the outer pull `next_batch()`; see `Buffer::next_batch`
    /// for why the inherent door exists beside the trait method).
    pub fn next_batch(&mut self) -> Option<RowsBatch<R>> {
        if self.sent { return Option::None; }
        self.sent = true;
        return Option::Some(self.rows);
    }
}

// One packet, then exhaustion — including the EMPTY delta, which still ticks once.
impl<R> BatchStream<RowsBatch<R>> for Epoch<R> {
    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<RowsBatch<R>> { return self.next_batch(); }
}

// Row-major: the batch is `&[R]`, the caller's own slice (§2).
impl<R> RowMajor for Epoch<R> {}

// Re-presenting the same epoch is free — the rows are the caller's and were never
// consumed. This is what a non-`Rewind` STREAMING source cannot offer, and it is why
// the incremental tier's decline of a streaming source (`rexpr_walk::incr_eligible`,
// "a join source STREAMS") is a statement about the SOURCE and not about this type.
impl<R> Rewind for Epoch<R> {
    fn rewind(&mut self) {
        self.sent = false;
    }
}

// The delta's row count is the slice length — free, and unmoved by pulling.
impl<R> SizedStream for Epoch<R> {
    fn size(&self) -> u64 {
        return self.rows.len() as u64;
    }
}

// DELIBERATELY NOT `Bidirectional`/`RandomAccess`/`OrderedBy<K>`. A borrowed one-packet
// stream could implement the traversal chain as trivially as `Buffer` does (position
// arithmetic over a slice), and it is left off because nothing positions inside a delta:
// an epoch is applied whole, and a capability with no consumer is a claim no test can
// bite. `OrderedBy` is a different matter — a delta carries no order fact at all.
